WE ARE
The European External Action Service (EEAS) is the European Union’s diplomatic service. Since 2011, the EEAS carries out the EU’s Common Foreign and Security Policy (CFSP) to promote peace, prosperity, security, and the interests of the EU across the globe. It brings together European civil servants, diplomats from EU Member States, national experts and local staff worldwide.
The EEAS supports the High Representative in the exercise of her mandate to steer the Union’s CFSP, including the Common Security and Defence Policy (CSDP), to represent the EU on issues relating to the CFSP and to chair the Foreign Affairs Council. It also supports her as Vice-President of the Commission with regard to her responsibilities within the Commission, including the coordination of other aspects of the EU’s external action. The EEAS works in close cooperation with the EU Member States, the General Secretariat of the Council, the services of the Commission and the Secretariat General of the European Parliament.
The EEAS Headquarters are in Brussels. It is also responsible for the management of the network of 145 Union Delegations and Offices worldwide. These Diplomatic Representations play a vital role in representing the EU in the country where they are based, in promoting the values and interests of the EU, and in building networks and partnerships. They are responsible for all policy areas of the relationship between the EU and the host country/organisation.
The EU Situation Room is an EEAS Division directly attached to the EEAS Crisis Response Centre (GII.CRC) and operates as a permanent stand-by body, providing worldwide monitoring, early warning and situational awareness 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, all year round.
It acts as a situational information hub for relevant stakeholders across the European institutions, continuously monitoring open-source information, consolidating and dispatching crisis-related information provided by EU Delegations, EU Member States, EU CSDP Operations and Missions, EUSR teams and international organisations. Relevant information is disseminated through dedicated media monitoring products, situation reports and other established information channels.
The EU Situation Room also serves as the EEAS operational switchboard, notably facilitating HRVP phone calls with counterparts worldwide, and acts as the first point of contact for information relating to crisis situations.
WE PROPOSE:
The EEAS is launching a call for expression of interest for the recruitment of one ASSISTANT (AST3) as Temporary Agent under Article 2(b) of the Conditions of Employment of other servants of the EU in accordance with EEAS Decision Admin(2015) 20 on the engagement and use of temporary agents.
Please note that, candidates who have been engaged by the EEAS as non-permanent servants (temporary and contract agents engaged under Article 3b of the CEOS), will be bound by Decision ADMIN(2023) 24 of the High Representative of the Union for Foreign Affairs and Security Policy of 14/07/2023 on the maximum duration of engagement by the European External Action Service of non-permanent staff under successive limited duration contracts of different types, and on the minimum lapse of time between successive contracts under Article 2(e) of the CEOS and repealing the Decision ADMIN(2020) 10 of the High Representative of the Union for Foreign Affairs and Security Policy of 16/07/2020.
WE LOOK FOR:
A dynamic, proactive and highly motivated colleague with very good communication and organisational skills and with a developed sense of service. He/she will be entrusted with the following main tasks:
contribute to the monitoring of world events 24 hours a day on the basis of open sources (major news networks, online media, etc.) and internal sources (e.g. EU Delegations, CSDP missions and operations) in order to identify events that are of political and/or security interest and alert the HRVP, the EEAS Secretary General, and other EEAS senior management officials thereof, as appropriate;
produce written information products (Daily Headlines, Crisis Situation Reports, etc.);
support setting up teleconferences for the HRVP, the Secretary General of the EEAS and other senior management officials, as appropriate;
send out alerts through messaging applications and/or SMS;
in times of crisis:
provide early warning and situational awareness;
serve as a contact point for monitoring the development of the situation and ensure communication with central services for Business Continuity (BC);
support the EEAS Crisis Response Mechanism, especially by supplying initial and continuous situational awareness reports;
provide support in case of the activation of the Integrated Political Crisis Response mechanism (IPCR);
promote regular contacts with national crisis response and crisis coordination counterparts of EU Member States as well as crisis management centres of other international and/or regional organisations;
maintain contacts with EEAS Staff in Brussels, EU Delegations and liaise closely with the EU Watchkeeping Capability on issues related to EU CSDP missions and operations;
support other EEAS services and delegations (Medical service, Field security, GALILEO alerts etc.);
carry out ad hoc and routine EU SITUATION ROOM tasks as required;
be operational 24 hours a day, 7 days a week (the Duty Officer function is operated on a roster basis);
contribute to the organisation of the duty roster in order to guarantee 24/7 service.
In order to work in the EU SITUATION ROOM, a security clearance at minimum level EU SECRET is required.
Specific requirements:
The EU Situation Room Duty Officers work in 24/7 shifts, on a continuous basis, all around the year. Currently, dayshifts start at 07:00 and finish at 19:00 and nightshifts start at 19:00 and finish at 07:00. Weekly rotation of shifts runs from Wednesday to Wednesday. The yearly number of working hours for a Duty officer is the same as for all staff (equivalent to 8 hours per day). For the work out of office hours (night shift, weekends and official holiday periods), a specific compensation is foreseen in accordance with the EU Staff Regulations.
The recruited person undertakes the responsibility to join one of the teams of Duty Officers and agree a yearly shift plan, formalised in the duty roster.
